 Yes. There is a line designated with the text **\"-OHE-\"** running horizontally along the bottom boundary (southern edge) of the site plan, indicating an overhead electric line. There are also small circular symbols with \"x\" marks inside them located at intervals along this line, which conventionally represent the utility poles.\n*   **Are they depicted in relation to proposed tree locations?** Yes. Numerous proposed trees (depicted by large circular canopy symbols with center marks) are shown throughout the plan, including several planted directly adjacent to and underneath the **\"-OHE-\"** line along the bottom boundary. Some of these nearby trees are labeled with tags such as **\"M\"** (which the Key Notes define as \"M - MITIGATION TREE\").\n*   **Can clearance distances be measured?** Yes, horizontal clearance distances can be measured. Because the overhead electric line and the center points (trunks) of the proposed trees are shown together on a single plan that includes a graphic scale (labeled **\"SCALE IN FEET (1\"=20'-0\")\"** in the bottom right corner), a reviewer can measure the horizontal distance between the tree locations and the overhead conductors. *(Note: As this is a 2D plan view, only horizontal clearances can be measured, not vertical).*"}]}
